    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Plotnine Chart\"\nexecute:\n  warning: false\n  error: false\n\nformat:\n  html:\n    toc: true\n    toc-location: right\n    code-fold: show\n    code-tools: true\n    number-sections: true\n    code-block-bg: true\n    code-block-border-left: \"#31BAE9\"\n---\n\n![](images/logo-540.png){width=\"300\"}\n\n# Introduction to Plotnine\n\nPlotnine is a Python implementation of the Grammar of Graphics, inspired by R's `ggplot2`. It allows you to create complex and beautiful plots by adding layers of data, aesthetics, and geoms. This document provides a guide to creating various plots with Plotnine.\n\n::: {#1bb870b9 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport plotnine\nprint(plotnine.__version__)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\n0.14.5\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#c526947c .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfrom plotnine import *\nimport seaborn as sns\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Load an example dataset\ntips = sns.load_dataset(\"tips\")\ntips.head()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>total_bill</th>\n      <th>tip</th>\n      <th>sex</th>\n      <th>smoker</th>\n      <th>day</th>\n      <th>time</th>\n      <th>size</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>16.99</td>\n      <td>1.01</td>\n      <td>Female</td>\n      <td>No</td>\n      <td>Sun</td>\n      <td>Dinner</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>10.34</td>\n      <td>1.66</td>\n      <td>Male</td>\n      <td>No</td>\n      <td>Sun</td>\n      <td>Dinner</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>21.01</td>\n      <td>3.50</td>\n      <td>Male</td>\n      <td>No</td>\n      <td>Sun</td>\n      <td>Dinner</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>23.68</td>\n      <td>3.31</td>\n      <td>Male</td>\n      <td>No</td>\n      <td>Sun</td>\n      <td>Dinner</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>24.59</td>\n      <td>3.61</td>\n      <td>Female</td>\n      <td>No</td>\n      <td>Sun</td>\n      <td>Dinner</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n# Scatter Plot\n\nA scatter plot is used to visualize the relationship between two continuous variables.\n\n::: {#87ddf5f8 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", y=\"total_bill\") + geom_point()\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-4-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Color by Group\n\nYou can color the points based on a categorical variable to see how the relationship varies across different groups.\n\n::: {#5d9dd49b .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", y=\"total_bill\") + geom_point(aes(color=\"sex\"))\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-5-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Size by Group\n\nThe size of the points can be mapped to a variable to add another dimension to the plot.\n\n::: {#7d9fb918 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", y=\"total_bill\", size=\"size\") + geom_point()\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-6-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n# Line Plot\n\nA line plot is suitable for showing the trend of a variable over time.\n\n::: {#0e78827f .cell execution_count=6}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ndowjones = sns.load_dataset(\"dowjones\")\ndowjones.head()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=6}\n```{=html}\n<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Date</th>\n      <th>Price</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>1914-12-01</td>\n      <td>55.00</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>1915-01-01</td>\n      <td>56.55</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>1915-02-01</td>\n      <td>56.00</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>1915-03-01</td>\n      <td>58.30</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>1915-04-01</td>\n      <td>66.45</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#f9d0ad85 .cell execution_count=7}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=dowjones) + aes(x=\"Date\", y=\"Price\") + geom_line()\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-8-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Line Plot with Dots\nYou can also add markers to the line plot to highlight the data points.\n\n::: {#005fa068 .cell execution_count=8}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=dowjones) + aes(x=\"Date\", y=\"Price\") + geom_line() + geom_point()\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-9-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#825c288f .cell execution_count=9}\n``` {.python .cell-code code-fold=\"true\"}\nimport random\n\n# Create datasets for comparison\ndowjones2 = dowjones.copy()\ndowjones2['type'] = 'old'\n\ndowjones3 = dowjones.copy()\ndowjones3['Price'] = dowjones3['Price'] + random.random() * 200\ndowjones3['type'] = 'new'\n\ndowjones4 = pd.concat([dowjones2, dowjones3], ignore_index=True)\ndowjones4 = dowjones4.sort_values('Date').reset_index(drop=True)\n```\n:::\n\n\n::: {#c441548e .cell execution_count=10}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ndowjones4.head()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=10}\n```{=html}\n<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Date</th>\n      <th>Price</th>\n      <th>type</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>1914-12-01</td>\n      <td>55.000000</td>\n      <td>old</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>1914-12-01</td>\n      <td>57.601244</td>\n      <td>new</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>1915-01-01</td>\n      <td>59.151244</td>\n      <td>new</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>1915-01-01</td>\n      <td>56.550000</td>\n      <td>old</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>1915-02-01</td>\n      <td>56.000000</td>\n      <td>old</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Color by Group\n\nYou can plot multiple lines on the same plot to compare different groups.\n\n::: {#8082adf3 .cell execution_count=11}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=dowjones4) + aes(x=\"Date\", y=\"Price\") + geom_line(aes(color=\"type\"))\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-12-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n# Histogram\n\nA histogram visualizes the distribution of a single continuous variable.\n\n::: {#97525069 .cell execution_count=12}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\") + geom_histogram()\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-13-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Color by Group\n\nYou can create separate histograms for different groups to compare their distributions.\n\n::: {#3ac07318 .cell execution_count=13}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", fill='sex') + geom_histogram(position='dodge')\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-14-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n# Bar Chart\n\nA bar chart is used to display categorical data with bars of lengths proportional to the values they represent.\n\n::: {#704b88d6 .cell execution_count=14}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x='sex', y='tip', fill=\"sex\") + geom_col()\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-15-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n# Box Plot\n\nA box plot shows the distribution of a dataset, including the median, quartiles, and potential outliers.\n\n::: {#d88de3aa .cell execution_count=15}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x='day', y='tip', fill=\"day\") + geom_boxplot()\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-16-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Color by Group\n\nYou can group box plots by a categorical variable to compare distributions.\n\n::: {#624af765 .cell execution_count=16}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x='day', y='tip', fill=\"sex\") + geom_boxplot()\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-17-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n# Strip Plot\n\nA strip plot is a scatter plot for a categorical variable, showing the distribution of data points.\n\n::: {#d574613c .cell execution_count=17}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x='day', y='tip') + geom_jitter(width=0.1)\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-18-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Color by Group\n\nYou can color the points in a strip plot to distinguish between different groups.\n\n::: {#7ee0b7dd .cell execution_count=18}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x='day', y='tip', fill=\"sex\") + geom_jitter(position=position_jitterdodge())\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-19-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n# Facet Plot\n\nFacet plots create subplots for different subsets of the data, allowing for easy comparison.\n\n::: {#42eb23ba .cell execution_count=19}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", y=\"total_bill\") + geom_point(aes(color=\"sex\"))\n    + facet_wrap(\"day\")\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-20-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Three Plots per Row\n\nYou can control the layout of the facet grid by specifying the number of columns.\n\n::: {#4b9174cd .cell execution_count=20}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", y=\"total_bill\") + geom_point(aes(color=\"sex\"))\n    + facet_wrap(\"day\", ncol=3)\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-21-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n# Customizing Title, Size, and Axis Names\n\n## Add Title\n\nYou can add a title to your plot to provide context.\n\n::: {#0aba0eee .cell execution_count=21}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", fill='sex') + geom_histogram(position='dodge') + ggtitle(\"Tip by Sex\")\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-22-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Adjust Size\n\nThe size of the plot can be customized to fit your needs.\n\n::: {#92ac8f4a .cell execution_count=22}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", fill='sex') + geom_histogram(position='dodge') + ggtitle(\"Tip by Sex\") + theme(figure_size=(4, 3))\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-23-output-1.png){width=384 height=288}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Change Axis Names\n\nYou can change the labels of the x and y axes for clarity.\n\n::: {#53c1c1c6 .cell execution_count=23}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", y=\"total_bill\") + geom_point() + scale_x_continuous(name=\"New X Name\") + scale_y_continuous(name=\"New Y Name\")\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-24-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n# Applying Themes\n\nPlotnine offers several built-in themes to change the appearance of your plots. You can find all themes [here](https://github.com/has2k1/plotnine/tree/main/plotnine/themes).\n\n::: {.panel-tabset .nav-pills}\n\n## xkcd Theme\n\nThis theme gives your plots a hand-drawn, xkcd-style look.\n\n::: {#ce194a5a .cell execution_count=24}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", fill='sex') + geom_histogram(position='dodge') + theme_xkcd()\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-25-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## theme_538\n\nThis theme mimics the style of the FiveThirtyEight website.\n\n::: {#471d58be .cell execution_count=25}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", fill='sex') + geom_histogram(position='dodge') + theme_538()\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-26-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## theme_dark\n\nThis theme uses a dark background for a modern look.\n\n::: {#c0adca33 .cell execution_count=26}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", fill='sex') + geom_histogram(position='dodge') + theme_dark()\n)\np\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](2 plotnine_files/figure-html/cell-27-output-1.png){width=672 height=480}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n:::\n\n# Saving a Plot\n\nYou can save your plot to a file in various formats.\n\n::: {#6b7637ef .cell execution_count=27}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\np = (\n    ggplot(data=tips) + aes(x=\"tip\", fill='sex') + geom_histogram(position='dodge') + theme_dark()\n)\np.save(filename='test3.png')\n```\n:::\n\n\n# Animated Plot\n\nPlotnine can also be used to create animated plots.\n\n::: {#43734ec4 .cell execution_count=28}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfrom plotnine.animation import PlotnineAnimation\n```\n:::\n\n\n::: {#2d11b56b .cell execution_count=29}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nnew_data = dowjones4.sample(50, random_state=42)\nnew_data = new_data.sort_values(by=['Date'], ascending=True)\n\ndef plot(x):\n    df2 = dowjones4.query('Date <= @x')\n    p = (\n        ggplot(df2, aes(x='Date', y='Price'))\n        + geom_line(aes(color=\"type\"))\n        + theme(subplots_adjust={'right': 0.85})\n    )\n    return p\n\nplots = (plot(i) for i in new_data[\"Date\"])\n```\n:::\n\n\n::: {#3bf0b8b5 .cell execution_count=30}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfrom matplotlib import rc\n\nrc(\"animation\", html=\"html5\")\n\nanimation = PlotnineAnimation(plots, interval=300, repeat_delay=500)\nanimation\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=30}\n```{=html}\n<video width=\"672\" height=\"480\" controls autoplay loop>\n  <source type=\"video/mp4\" src=\"data:video/mp4;base64,AAAAIGZ0eXBNNFYgAAACAE00ViBpc29taXNvMmF2YzEAAAAIZnJlZQAATohtZGF0AAACrgYF//+q\n3EXpvebZSLeWLNgg2SPu73gyNjQgL